## 11. WHY THE PLAYER WAS CHOSEN

Inside the academy, the protagonist meets the program director.

The director explains that PROJECT ABSIDIAN has been developing technologies for a future lunar settlement.

However, the systems compete for limited resources.

Oxygen production consumes electricity.

Water recycling requires functioning equipment.

Greenhouses need water, light, and energy.

Scientific instruments create additional electrical demand.

Radiation protection imposes operational constraints.

The program needs someone who understands how these interconnected systems affect one another.

The protagonist's successful response to the blackout demonstrated precisely that skill.

The director explains:

"We don't need someone who can make one system perfect."

"We need someone who understands what happens when every system depends on another."

The protagonist is invited to complete a special astronaut preparation program.

They are not immediately appointed mission commander.

Their responsibilities increase as they successfully complete training and demonstrate engineering judgment.

